[ 2026-10-05 06:20:37,878 ] groq._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/openai/v1/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b2dc0efa-54c9-4c14-96fc-454b74978dd1', 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are a mechanical engineering result formatter.\nAnalyze the conversation history, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n\nOriginal Parameters: {'mass_a': 20.0, 'mass_b': 10.0, 'mass_pulley': 6.0, 'radius_pulley': 0.2, 'friction_coefficient': 0.25, 'applied_force': 35.0, 'distance': 2.0, 'gravity': 9.81}\nOriginal Units: {'mass_a': 'kg', 'mass_b': 'kg', 'mass_pulley': 'kg', 'radius_pulley': 'm', 'friction_coefficient': '', 'applied_force': 'N', 'distance': 'm', 'gravity': 'm/s^2'}\nTarget Operations: ['force', 'mass', 'acceleration', 'torque', 'work', 'energy']\n\nExtract and generate:\n1. 'selected_tool': List of tool names used during the execution.\n2. 'calculation_result': Dictionary containing calculated outputs paired with their final units.\n3. 'validation_result': Dictionary with validation status e.g., {'valid': True, 'warnings': []}.\n4. 'explanation': Clear step-by-step technical explanation including formulas used, unit conversions, and final results."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "A 20 kg block A rests on a rough horizontal table and is connected by a massless, inextensible cable to a 10 kg block B hanging vertically over a solid-disk pulley. The pulley has a mass of 6 kg and a radius of 0.20 m, with moment of inertia I = (1/2)MR². The coefficient of kinetic friction between block A and the table is 0.25. A constant horizontal force of 35 N is applied to block A in the direction away from the pulley. The cable does not slip on the pulley, and the pulley axle is frictionless. The system starts from rest and block A moves 2 m toward the pulley. Take g = 9.81 m/s².\n\nDetermine the direction of motion, the linear acceleration of blocks A and B, the angular acceleration of the pulley, the tension in each side of the cable, the kinetic friction force, the final velocity of the blocks, the work done by the applied force, the work done by friction, the change in kinetic energy of the system, and the final rotational kinetic energy of the pulley. Show the governing equations and verify the results using Newton's second law, the rotational equation of motion, the no-slip constraint, and the work-energy principle."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': None, 'tool_calls': [{'type': 'function', 'id': 'fc_1f3c7733-0ba5-46f3-abcd-a9582927b9e2', 'function': {'name': 'calculate_force', 'arguments': '{"acceleration": 0.52037, "mass": 20}'}}]}, {'role': 'tool', 'content': '10.407399999999999', 'tool_call_id': 'fc_1f3c7733-0ba5-46f3-abcd-a9582927b9e2'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '**1.  Geometry and data**\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Value | Unit |\n|--------|---------|-------|------|\n| \\(m_A\\) | mass of block\u202fA | 20 | kg |\n| \\(m_B\\) | mass of block\u202fB | 10 | kg |\n| \\(m_p\\) | mass of pulley | 6 | kg |\n| \\(R\\)   | pulley radius | 0.20 | m |\n| \\(I\\)   | pulley moment of inertia \\(\\displaystyle I=\\frac12 m_pR^2\\) | \\(0.12\\) | kg·m² |\n| \\(\\mu_k\\) | coefficient of kinetic friction (A‑table) | 0.25 | – |\n| \\(F_{\\text{app}}\\) | horizontal force on A (away from pulley) | 35 | N |\n| \\(g\\)   | gravitational acceleration | 9.81 | m·s⁻² |\n| \\(s\\)   | distance travelled by A (and B) | 2.00 | m |\n\nFriction on block\u202fA  \n\n\\[\nf_k =\\mu_k N =\\mu_k m_A g =0.25(20)(9.81)=49.05\\;\\text{N}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Free‑body equations  \n\nTake **positive** direction **toward the pulley** for block\u202fA (left) and **downward** for block\u202fB.\n\n| Body | Equation (ΣF = ma) |\n|------|--------------------|\n| **Block\u202fA** | \\(T_1 - F_{\\text{app}} - f_k = m_A a\\)\u2003\u2003(1) |\n| **Block\u202fB** | \\(m_B g - T_2 = m_B a\\)\u2003\u2003(2) |\n| **Pulley** (no‑slip, \\(a = \\alpha R\\)) | \\((T_2 - T_1)R = I\\alpha = I\\frac{a}{R}\\)\u2003\u2003(3) |\n\nFrom (3)  \n\n\\[\nT_2 - T_1 = \\frac{I}{R^{2}}\\,a = \\frac{0.12}{0.20^{2}}a = 3a \\qquad\\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Solve for the linear acceleration  \n\nFrom (1)  \n\n\\[\nT_1 = m_A a + F_{\\text{app}} + f_k = 20a + 35 + 49.05 = 20a + 84.05 \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)  \n\n\\[\nT_2 = m_B g - m_B a = 98.10 - 10a \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert (5) and (6) into (4):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(98.10 - 10a\\bigr) - \\bigl(20a + 84.05\\bigr) = 3a\n\\]\n\n\\[\n14.05 -30a = 3a\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;33a = 14.05\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a = 0.426\\ \\text{m·s}^{-2}}\n\\]\n\nThe **positive sign** confirms that block\u202fA moves **toward the pulley** and block\u202fB moves **downward**.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Angular acceleration of the pulley  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\alpha = \\frac{a}{R}= \\frac{0.426}{0.20}=2.13\\ \\text{rad·s}^{-2}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Tensions in the rope  \n\nUsing (5) and (6) with \\(a =0.426\\; \\text{m·s}^{-2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_1 &= 20(0.426)+84.05 = 92.6\\ \\text{N}\\\\[2mm]\nT_2 &= 98.10-10(0.426)= 93.8\\ \\text{N}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{T_1 = 9.26\\times10^{1}\\ \\text{N}},\\qquad \n\\boxed{T_2 = 9.38\\times10^{1}\\ \\text{N}}\n\\]\n\n(The larger tension on the B‑side produces the clockwise torque that rotates the pulley.)\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Kinetic‑friction force (already computed)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f_k = 49.1\\ \\text{N}}\\quad\\text{(acts to the right, opposite the motion of A)}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final linear speed after moving 2\u202fm  \n\nStarting from rest, constant acceleration \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\nv = \\sqrt{2as}= \\sqrt{2(0.426)(2.00)} = \\sqrt{1.704}=1.30\\ \\text{m·s}^{-1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{v = 1.30\\ \\text{m·s}^{-1}}\n\\]\n\n(The same speed applies to block\u202fB and to the rope.)\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Work of the external forces  \n\n*Applied horizontal force* (opposite to the displacement)\n\n\\[\nW_{F'}], 'model': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'n': 1, 'reasoning_effort': None, 'reasoning_format':